From 9a364b85a3a87c225ea6bff93266bf0e772a6e6e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 20:48:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Measure bytes per object, and stop guessing at item 2-3

Roadmap item 2-3 proposed removing the double storage every shape-tracked
object carries - its value lives in both shapeSlots and the PropertySequence -
described as a pure removal justified by "storing twice and paying to keep them
in sync". Neither half of that survives measurement, and measuring it needed an
instrument the repository did not have.

It is not a pure removal. The two stores serve different access paths: a cached
read takes shapeSlots[slot], an array index, while the generic path takes
ownProperties, a radix trie keyed by name. Removing the array puts a trie walk
back on the path phases C and D exist to keep off it; removing the value from
the trie puts a shape lookup on every generic read, descriptor query and
enumeration. A cost probe that dropped only the trie write proved it directly -
the store loop stayed correct because it read through the cache, and a later
cold read of the same property returned the stale value.

The throughput case is ~3%, of the most favourable workload available: same
build, one line differing, four interleaved pairs of a 20 M-iteration pure
overwrite loop, median of paired ratios 0.971, every pair the same direction.
And most of what the item was aiming at is already collected - it was written
when a store cost two key lookups, and P1-3 and the transition entry removed
the second from both cached paths.

So it is a memory item, which is what --object-alloc measures: bytes per object
by construction form and field count, by Appendix A's method - forced gen2
collection, then GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread() deltas over 50 000
objects, warmed first so compilation, key strings, shapes and cache entries
land outside the measured run. Field values are small integer constants the
per-thread cache serves without allocating, so a difference between two rows is
structure rather than contents, and the empty rows are the baseline the
per-field cost is measured against.

The slot array turns out to be 4.5% of a constructor-built three-field object.
What the same run found instead is that one field costs the same as three -
1256 B either way, about 1040 B more than an object with none. The per-object
cost is a fixed block: SAUint32Map allocates trie nodes from a VirtualMemory
whose first allocation rounds up to sixteen, and a node is a whole descriptor
plus two link fields, so one property reserves sixteen descriptors' worth of
memory and uses one. That block covers the first four node groups, which is why
fields two and three are free.

Shrinking the floor is a trade rather than a win, which the emitter also
settles: rounding to four instead of sixteen makes a one-field object 53%
cheaper and a three-field object 16% cheaper while making an eight-field object
27% more expensive, and a minimum of four with capacity doubling is worse again
at 43%. Choosing a floor needs the size distribution of real objects, which no
synthetic probe can supply and an Octane run can. Recorded on the roadmap as an
item with its measurement named, not started.
